export const KNOWLEDGE = "# KoKreaters knowledge base (kokreaters.com)\n\n## About KoKreaters\nKoKreaters is an independent technical and strategy consultancy for food, feed and specialty ingredients. KoKreaters helps food, nutrition and specialty-ingredient businesses design better products, run better plants and make bolder decisions, with 25 years of plant-to-boardroom leadership behind every recommendation.\nFounder: Ganeaysh Kulkarni. Ganeaysh Kulkarni is the founder of KoKreaters. A food technologist with 25 years at Wilmar and Cargill, he has built specialty fats facilities, commissioned refineries, led global R&D and FSQR teams and supported acquisitions across Asia, Europe and the Americas. He now helps food and ingredient companies co-create better products, plants and decisions.\nKoKreaters is the consultancy Ganeaysh Kulkarni wished he could call when he was running plants and R&D teams: someone who has done the work, understands the business and builds the answer with you.\nPositioning: Deep expertise, shared ownership, measurable results. We do not hand over a report and leave. We build the answer with your people, so it still works after we have gone.\nWhat makes KoKreaters different is range. Most consultants are either plant engineers or strategists. KoKreaters works from the plant floor to the boardroom: designing a product, fixing a process, passing a customer audit, valuing an acquisition and writing the business plan, all with the same person who has done it inside global companies.\nBase: Based in the Netherlands, working worldwide. Countries of the founder's career: India, Indonesia, Ukraine, Türkiye, the Netherlands.\nThe name: Ko stands for together: co-creation, collaboration, community. Kreaters are the people who make things happen. The tagline in the logo spells out the method: Connect · Collaborate · Create.\nFounder quote: “Co-creation and creativity are at the soul of everything we do. We do not just advise. We create with you.” (Ganeaysh Kulkarni, Founder)\nTrack record (from the founder's career): 25 years in oils, fats and food ingredients; 5 countries of plant and operational leadership; 3 regions of teams led: Americas, EMEA, Asia-Pacific; WHO Director-General recognition for trans-fat elimination work.\n\n## Expertise: the six areas\nMost problems in food and ingredients do not respect departmental lines. A texture defect may start in the refinery; an audit failure may start in the business model. Our six areas of expertise are designed to work together.\n\n### 1. Product and Category Innovation (/expertise/product-innovation)\nPromise: From unmet need to a product that scales.\nWe turn consumer and customer needs into clear technical briefs, then into products your plants can make profitably. Our roots are in oils and fats, so we are at our best where fat functionality decides whether a product succeeds: texture, bloom, shelf life, mouthfeel and cost.\nWhat we help with:\n- Specialty fats: cocoa butter equivalents (CBE), confectionery and filling fats, bakery shortenings, margarines and laminating fats\n- Plant-based, dairy and specialised nutrition applications\n- Reformulation and renovation: trans-fat elimination, saturated fat reduction, cleaner labels, cost-in-use\n- Sensory evaluation, consumer insight and foresight\n- Technical road-mapping and innovation pipelines\nTypical deliverables: Innovation roadmaps, technical briefs, formulation and prototype plans, lab-to-plant scale-up plans, launch support.\n\n### 2. Process Design and Plant Performance (/expertise/process-design)\nPromise: Plants that run better, cost less and grow with you.\nWe have designed, commissioned and run oils and fats plants in four very different countries, from tropical Indonesia to a refinery operating at −25 °C in Ukraine. We bring that experience to your process, whether you are building new, expanding or fixing what you have.\nWhat we help with:\n- Refining, fractionation, interesterification and blending process design\n- Greenfield and brownfield projects, commissioning and start-up\n- Debottlenecking: throughput, yield, utilities and cost\n- Technology scouting and transfer, including enzymatic processing\n- Asset-light and tolling models for faster, lower-capital growth\nTypical deliverables: Process design basis, plant assessments, debottlenecking plans, commissioning support, technology transfer packages.\n\n### 3. Food Safety, Quality and Contaminants (/expertise/food-safety-quality)\nPromise: Pass the audit. Protect the brand. Stay ahead of the regulation.\nCertification covers the generic food safety system. Multinational customers look further, at the product- and process-specific risks. With eight years leading FSQR teams at Cargill, we help suppliers see their plant through the auditor’s eyes, and help brand owners understand their suppliers.\nWhat we help with:\n- Process contaminant mitigation: 3-MCPD and glycidyl esters, trans fats, mineral oil hydrocarbons\n- Plant and supplier audits; readiness for multinational customer pre-approval audits\n- Building FSQR systems, teams and culture\n- Packaging-related contaminants, product integrity and shelf life\n- Regulatory horizon scanning for India and Europe\nTypical deliverables: Audit reports with photographic evidence, gap analyses, corrective action plans, mitigation programmes, training.\n\n### 4. Strategy, Feasibility and Market Entry (/expertise/strategy-feasibility)\nPromise: Clear answers for Go/No-Go decisions.\nBefore you commit capital, you need to know whether the market is real, whether the technology works and whether the numbers hold. We combine market analysis with technical judgement to give boards and investors a decision they can defend.\nWhat we help with:\n- Market analysis, product-market fit and competitive landscapes\n- Feasibility studies and business plans for new plants and product lines\n- Market entry and go-to-market strategy, including India and Asia\n- Business–technology alignment plans and technical roadmaps\n- Investor-grade reports and presentations\nTypical deliverables: Feasibility reports, business plans with scenarios, market entry strategies, investor decks.\n\n### 5. M&A, Due Diligence and Partnerships (/expertise/due-diligence)\nPromise: See what is really behind the deal.\nSpreadsheets do not show a tired refinery, a fragile quality system or a technology that will not transfer. We give buyers, investors and partners an independent technical view, then help make the integration work.\nWhat we help with:\n- Technical due diligence of plants, products, R&D and quality systems\n- Post-acquisition integration planning\n- Joint ventures, partner and channel selection\n- Technology licensing and transfer assessments\nTypical deliverables: Due diligence reports, red-flag summaries, integration plans, partner assessments.\n\n### 6. Capability, Leadership and Transformation (/expertise/capability)\nPromise: Leave stronger teams behind.\nLasting change comes from people. We train technical teams, transfer know-how between sites and coach leaders through growth and change, drawing on experience leading teams across the Americas, EMEA and Asia-Pacific.\nWhat we help with:\n- Technical training: oils and fats, specialty fats, applications, FSQR\n- Technology transfer and site start-up coaching\n- Leadership development for technical leaders\n- Organisation design for R&D, technical services and quality\n- Interim technical leadership\nTypical deliverables: Training programmes, academies, transfer playbooks, organisation designs, interim roles.\n\n## Industries (/industries)\nOur roots are in edible oils and specialty fats, so we are at our best where fat functionality decides whether a product succeeds: texture, bloom, shelf life, mouthfeel and cost. That knowledge runs from the refinery into confectionery, bakery, nutrition, plant-based foods and snacks, and on into personal care, pet food and the inputs the oils industry depends on. Each of the nine industries below links to the expertise it draws on most.\n- Confectionery: Cocoa butter equivalents, filling and coating fats, bloom resistance and cost-in-use for chocolate and sugar confectionery. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Bakery: Shortenings, margarines and laminating fats that perform on the line and in the bite, with lower trans and saturated fat. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Dairy and specialised nutrition: Fat systems and contaminant control for infant, medical and adult nutrition, where quality has no margin. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Food Safety, Quality and Contaminants.\n- Plant-based: Fat functionality that delivers the melt, bite and juiciness consumers expect. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation.\n- Snacks and food service: Frying oils, stability and cost for high-volume operations. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Edible oils and specialty fats: Refining, fractionation and specialty fats, from crude oil to customer specification. Relevant expertise: Process Design and Plant Performance, Food Safety, Quality and Contaminants.\n- Personal care and oleochemicals: Ingredient functionality and manufacturing processes for beauty and oleochemical products. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Pet food and animal nutrition: Product development and process improvement tailored to species-specific needs and regulations. Relevant expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Industrial minerals and process inputs: Market entry, feasibility and application know-how for the inputs the oils industry depends on, such as bleaching earths. Relevant expertise: Strategy, Feasibility and Market Entry.\n\n## Approach (/approach): how we work\nWe create with you, not for you. The best answers come from people who know the plant, the product and the market. Our role is to connect that knowledge, add 25 years of global experience, and help your teams create something that lasts.\nThe method: Connect. Collaborate. Create.\n### 1. Connect\nWe start where the problem lives: on your plant floor, in your lab, with your customers. We listen first, then diagnose.\nWhat we do: Plant observation, conversations with your teams and a review of your data, before any recommendation.\nWhat you get: A written diagnosis with the evidence behind it, such as an audit report or gap analysis, and a proposed scope for the next step.\nTypical duration: Typically 2–5 days on site, with the report within two weeks.\n### 2. Collaborate\nWe design the answer with your teams, not around them, so the people who will run it own it.\nWhat we do: Working sessions, trials and design reviews with your teams, from the plant floor to the boardroom.\nWhat you get: An answer your team helped design and owns, such as a process design basis, a formulation plan or a business plan.\nTypical duration: Typically 4–16 weeks, as a project with a fixed scope.\n### 3. Create\nWe build, test and scale the solution, then hand it over with the measures to prove it works.\nWhat we do: Scale-up, commissioning and launch support, training for the people who will run the solution, and a structured handover.\nWhat you get: A solution in operation, a team that can run it without us, and agreed measures that show the result.\nTypical duration: Typically half a day to two days of training, then a set number of days each month if you want ongoing support.\n### Principles\n- Plant floor to boardroom: We are as comfortable in a control room as in a board meeting, and we connect the two.\n- Your team owns the result: We build capability as we go, so you do not depend on us afterwards.\n- Global playbook, local execution: Lessons from Asia, Europe and the Americas, adapted to your market, your raw materials and your people.\n- Evidence before opinion: Data, trials and plant observation first; recommendations second.\n- Confidential by default: Your formulations, processes and plans stay yours. We sign NDAs as standard.\n### Ways to work with us (engagement models)\n- Diagnostic or audit: 2–5 days on site, report within two weeks. Good for: Plant audits, customer pre-approval readiness, contaminant risk reviews.\n- Project: Fixed scope, 4–16 weeks. Good for: New products or processes, feasibility studies, business plans, due diligence.\n- Advisory retainer: A set number of days each month. Good for: Ongoing technical and strategic counsel, R&D steering, board advisory.\n- Interim leadership: Part-time or full-time for a defined period. Good for: Covering a technical director or R&D lead role.\n- Workshops and training: Half a day to two days. Good for: Oils and fats academies, innovation sprints, technology transfer.\n### Frequently asked questions\nQ: Do you sign an NDA?\nA: Yes. Your formulations, processes and plans stay yours. We sign NDAs as standard. We are happy to sign yours before the first detailed conversation.\nQ: How are fees agreed?\nA: We agree the scope and fee with you before any work starts.\nQ: Do you work on site or remotely?\nA: Both, depending on the work. We start where the problem lives: on your plant floor, in your lab, with your customers. A diagnostic or audit typically means 2–5 days on site, with the report within two weeks. For longer engagements, the balance of site visits and remote work is agreed with you in the scope.\nQ: Which languages do you work in?\nA: Ganeaysh works in English, Hindi and Marathi, with conversational Bahasa Indonesia and basic Russian.\nQ: Where are you based, and do you travel?\nA: We are based in the Netherlands and work with clients worldwide. Ganeaysh’s career has taken him across India, Indonesia, Ukraine, Türkiye and the Netherlands. Travel for on-site work is agreed with you as part of the scope.\n\n## Impact (/impact)\nFrom India’s first integrated specialty fats facility to a refinery at −25 °C: the results behind KoKreaters.\nResults from the founder’s career (at Wilmar and Cargill, before KoKreaters; never present them as KoKreaters client projects):\n- India · Wilmar: India’s first world-class integrated specialty fats facility. Challenge: Create integrated specialty fats production in India, where no facility of its kind yet existed. What was done: Built and activated the facility. Result: India’s first world-class integrated specialty fats facility. Expertise: Product and Category Innovation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Indonesia · Wilmar: 21% growth in Indonesia. Challenge: Turn manufacturing operations in Indonesia into a source of growth for the business. What was done: Reshaped manufacturing operations. Result: 21% growth. Expertise: Capability, Leadership and Transformation, Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Ukraine · Wilmar: Ukraine’s first cold-climate crude palm oil refinery. Challenge: Design and build a continuous refinery able to operate in extreme winter conditions, down to −25 °C. What was done: Full design-to-execution leadership of the facility, from process design to commissioning. Result: The first refinery of its kind in Ukraine; overall cost structure improved by 15%. Expertise: Process Design and Plant Performance.\n- Cargill: Trans-fat elimination work recognised by the WHO Director-General. Challenge: Eliminate industrially produced trans fat from oils and fats through reformulation. What was done: Trans-fat (iTFA) elimination work. Result: Publicly recognised by the Director-General of the World Health Organization. Expertise: Food Safety, Quality and Contaminants, Product and Category Innovation.\n\n## Founder (/about): Ganeaysh Kulkarni\nGaneaysh Kulkarni founded KoKreaters in 2026 after 25 years building and leading technical organisations in edible oils, specialty fats and food ingredients.\nHe began his career at Wilmar in 2001, rising from Technical Head Specialties to Technical Director. There he built and activated India’s first world-class integrated specialty fats facility, reshaped manufacturing operations in Indonesia to deliver 21% growth, and commissioned Ukraine’s first cold-climate crude palm oil refinery, operating at −25 °C and improving the cost structure by 15%.\nIn 2012 he joined Cargill. Over the next fourteen years he led Technical Services, Applications, R&D and Food Safety, Quality and Regulatory (FSQR) teams; served as Regional Technical Lead and Bakery Technology Transfer Leader, supporting technical due diligence for acquisitions; led Category R&D for Confectionery and Plant-Based; and was Global Product and Process Design Director from 2023 to 2026. His trans-fat elimination work at Cargill was publicly recognised by the Director-General of the World Health Organization.\nHis career has taken him across India, Indonesia, Ukraine, Türkiye and the Netherlands, leading teams in the Americas, EMEA and Asia-Pacific and working with global leaders in nutrition, food, personal care and pet food.\nGaneaysh holds a postgraduate degree in Food Technology from CFTRI Mysore and a degree in Chemistry from the University of Pune, and has completed programmes in Creativity and Innovation at IIM Ahmedabad and the EDGE Founder’s Bootcamp at UC Berkeley. He works in English, Hindi and Marathi, with conversational Bahasa Indonesia and basic Russian.\nHe is based in the Netherlands and works with clients worldwide.\nCareer timeline: CFTRI Mysore 1999–2001 (Postgraduate degree in Food Technology); Wilmar 2001–2012 (Technical Head Specialties to Technical Director); Cargill 2012–2026 (Technical, R&D and FSQR leadership; Global Product and Process Design Director, 2023–2026); KoKreaters 2026 (Founder, based in the Netherlands).\nEducation: Postgraduate degree in Food Technology, CFTRI Mysore; Degree in Chemistry, University of Pune; Creativity and Innovation programme, IIM Ahmedabad; EDGE Founder’s Bootcamp, UC Berkeley.\nLanguages: English, Hindi, Marathi, Bahasa Indonesia (conversational), Russian (basic).\nWorking with global leaders in nutrition, food, personal care and pet food.\n\n## Insights (/insights)\n### 3-MCPD and glycidyl esters: a practical playbook for refiners (/insights/3-mcpd-glycidyl-esters-playbook)\nArea: Food Safety, Quality and Contaminants.\nStandfirst: Process contaminants are not a laboratory problem. They are made in the refinery, step by step, and they can be unmade the same way.\nKey takeaways:\n- 3-MCPD esters and glycidyl esters form in different ways. Manage them separately.\n- Agree the analytical method and sampling points with your customer first.\n- Act in order: raw material, degumming and neutralisation, bleaching, deodorisation, then post-refining.\n- Trial one change at a time and follow the oil through to finished product.\n- Give the programme one owner, and write every proven change into the procedures.\nMost refiners first meet 3-MCPD as a number on a customer’s specification sheet. A buyer asks for lower values. The laboratory reports a result. Someone in the plant is asked to fix it. Six months later the number has hardly moved, and everyone is frustrated.\n\nThe problem is rarely effort. It is where the effort goes. 3-MCPD esters and glycidyl esters are process contaminants. They form inside the refinery, from things that arrive with the crude oil and from the conditions we create to refine it. That means they can be managed like any other process outcome: by understanding where they form, measuring the right things, and changing the process in the right order.\n\nRegulators in Europe have set maximum levels, and multinational customers often specify their own, tighter limits. \n\nThis is the playbook we use.\n\nFirst, name the two problems\n\nThe two families are usually discussed together, but they behave differently. (3-MCPD: 3-monochloropropane-1,2-diol. GE: glycidyl esters. DAG: diacylglycerols. FFA: free fatty acids.)\n\n3-MCPD esters need three things: a source of chlorine, the glycerides of the oil, and heat. The chlorine-containing precursors arrive with the crude oil. They come from the fruit, the mill and the water used along the way.\n\nGlycidyl esters form mainly from diacylglycerols at the high temperatures used in deodorisation. How much forms depends heavily on temperature and time.\n\nThe difference matters. A refiner who treats them as one problem often fixes one and makes the other worse. Each needs its own levers and its own measures.\n\nAgree how you measure before you argue about results\n\nBefore changing the process, agree the analytical method with your customer. Several official methods exist, and they do not always give the same answer on the same sample. Plants and customers can spend months debating results when the real disagreement is about the method.\n\nSo the first step is simple. Write down which method, which laboratory and which sampling points count. Then build a baseline: crude oil, after bleaching, after deodorisation and finished product, over enough batches to see the spread, not only the average.\n\nThe four places to act\n\nExhibit: Where 3-MCPD esters and glycidyl esters can be reduced, in the order we act\n- Raw material and crude oil: Chlorine precursors, FFA and DAG\n- Degumming and neutralisation: Washing, acid choice, precursor removal\n- Bleaching: Earth type, dose and acidity\n- Deodorisation: Temperature and time; most GE forms here\n- Post-refining: Adsorbents or a second, milder step\n\n1. Raw material and crude oil. The cheapest contaminant is the one that never enters the plant. Fresh fruit handling, mill practice and the water used upstream all affect the chlorine precursors and the diacylglycerol content of the crude. Crude oil with high free fatty acids usually carries more diacylglycerols, and more diacylglycerols mean more potential for glycidyl esters. Where you can influence supply, specify it. Where you cannot, measure it and segregate it.\n\n2. Degumming and neutralisation. Washing the crude oil can remove part of the water-soluble chlorine precursors. Chemical neutralisation also lowers precursors, but it changes yield and effluent, so judge it on the whole cost, not only on contaminant values. Acid dosing in degumming deserves attention too: the choice and amount of acid can affect what forms later.\n\n3. Bleaching. Bleaching earth is not only there for colour. Its type, dose and acidity influence what forms downstream. A more acidic earth may give a beautiful colour and a worse 3-MCPD result. Change one variable at a time, and follow the oil all the way through deodorisation, because the effect of bleaching often shows only at the end.\n\n4. Deodorisation. This is where most glycidyl esters form, and where the temperature–time profile is the main lever. Lower temperatures and shorter residence times reduce them, but they also change free fatty acid stripping, colour and flavour. Some plants use two temperature zones: a short, hotter step, then a longer, cooler one for stripping. The right answer depends on the equipment you have, the oil you refine and the specification you must meet.\n\nWhere the numbers still miss a demanding specification, there are post-refining options, such as treatment with specific adsorbents or a second, milder deodorisation. They work, but they add cost. They belong at the end of the playbook, not the beginning.\n\n“A contaminant that forms in the process can be managed like any other process outcome.”\n\nWhere to start\n\nIf you are starting from a customer complaint, resist the urge to change everything at once. Begin with two weeks of disciplined measurement at every step. Rank the batches from best to worst and compare what was different about them: the crude oil, the bleaching earth, the deodoriser temperature, the time in each vessel. The best batches usually show you the first lever to pull. Trial that one change, measure it the same way, and only then move to the next.\n\nTreat it as a system, not a task\n\nThe levers sit in different departments. Procurement owns the crude. Production owns bleaching and deodorisation. Quality owns the laboratory. Sales owns the customer conversation. When the target belongs to only one of them, progress stalls.\n\nWhat works is one programme with one owner, one baseline and one set of measures, reviewed at the same rhythm as yield and cost. Each change is trialled, measured at every step and then written into the operating procedures, so the result survives a change of shift or a change of manager.\n\nWhat a good programme leaves behind\n\nA good mitigation programme does not end with a report. It leaves behind a baseline that everyone trusts, a short list of process changes proven in plant trials, updated specifications for incoming crude oil, and people on every shift who understand why the changes matter.\n\nThat is how we work on contaminant mitigation: on the plant floor, with your team, until the improvement is part of how the plant runs.\n\n### Trans-fat elimination: what it really takes inside a plant (/insights/trans-fat-elimination-inside-a-plant)\nArea: Product and Category Innovation.\nStandfirst: Removing partially hydrogenated oils is a formulation change, a process change and a people change. Plants that treat it as only the first one struggle.\nKey takeaways:\n- List the functions the old fat delivered before choosing a replacement.\n- Combine fractionation, interesterification, blending and structuring as the product needs.\n- Aim for the lowest saturated fat that still delivers the function.\n- Prove the formulation at line speed and through shelf life, with the customer.\n- Verify elimination with analysis, specifications and supplier control.\nTaking industrial trans fat out of a product sounds like a single decision. Stop buying partially hydrogenated oil, buy something else, update the label. In practice it is one of the most demanding changes a fats plant and its customers can make. Partially hydrogenated oils did several jobs at once, and every one of those jobs still has to be done.\n\nOur founder’s trans-fat elimination work at Cargill was publicly recognised by the Director-General of the World Health Organization. This article sets out what that kind of work involves inside a plant. (iTFA: industrially produced trans-fatty acids, formed mainly when vegetable oils are partially hydrogenated.)\n\nStart with what the old fat was doing\n\nPartial hydrogenation was popular for good reasons. It turned liquid oils into solid fats with a wide plastic range. It gave stability against oxidation. It crystallised in a way that gave margarines and shortenings a smooth texture, and bakery products their volume and bite. It was available, consistent and relatively cheap.\n\nSo the first question is not “what replaces it?”. It is “what was it doing in this product?”. A laminating fat for puff pastry, a frying oil for a snack line and a filling fat for biscuits need different answers. We start every reformulation by writing down the functions the fat must deliver, how each is measured and what the customer will accept.\n\nBring procurement and sales in early\n\nA new fat system often means new raw materials. Palm fractions, fully hydrogenated hard stocks and different liquid oils have their own supply chains, price movements and certification needs. If procurement learns about the change last, the plant may end up with a formulation it cannot buy reliably.\n\nSales teams need to know too. Customers will ask what changed, why, and what it means for their own products and labels. A short, technical and honest explanation, ready before the first trial delivery, protects the relationship.\n\nThe replacement toolbox\n\nThere is no single substitute. There is a toolbox, and most solutions combine several tools.\n\nExhibit: Common routes to solid fat functionality without partially hydrogenated oils\n\n| Route | What it does well | What to watch |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fractionation, for example palm stearin and mid-fractions | Natural hard fractions, no chemical change to the oil | Dependence on one raw material; crystallisation behaviour |\n| Interesterification, chemical or enzymatic | Tailors the melting profile from hard stock and liquid oils | Capacity, processing cost and process control |\n| Full hydrogenation, then interesterification | Hard stock with no trans fat | Extra process steps; how the ingredient must be declared |\n| Blending | Simple and flexible | A limited range of textures on its own |\n| Structuring and emulsifier systems | Can reduce the solid fat needed | Formulation complexity and cost |\n\nEach route has a cost. Fractionation depends on the raw material. Interesterification needs capacity and control. And almost every route that delivers solid fat without trans fat brings saturated fat with it.\n\nDo not swap one problem for another\n\nThis is the trap. The quickest way to rebuild solid fat functionality is to add saturated fat. Regulators and customers increasingly watch saturated fat as well, so a reformulation that removes trans fat but adds a large amount of saturated fat may not survive the next review.\n\nThe better target is the lowest saturated fat that still delivers the function. That needs a precise understanding of crystallisation: which crystal form the fat settles in, how fast, and how that changes with processing and storage. It is technical work, and it is where most of the value lies.\n\nWhat changes on the plant floor\n\nA new fat system rarely runs well on settings designed for the old one.\n\nOn margarine and shortening lines, cooling, working and resting conditions often need to change, because the new fat crystallises differently. Tempering and storage temperatures may need adjusting. Fractionation or interesterification capacity may need to be added or rescheduled. Raw material storage and handling change with the new blend.\n\nNone of this can be settled in the laboratory alone. The formulation has to be proven at line speed, on the real equipment, with the real operators, and then followed through shelf life.\n\n“A formulation is only finished when it runs on the line and lasts on the shelf.”\n\nWhat changes for the customer\n\nThe customer’s plant changes too. A bakery’s laminating line, a confectioner’s enrober and a snack fryer will each respond to the new fat in their own way. Application trials with the customer, on their equipment, turn a good formulation into an accepted one. Technical service teams need to be ready before the switch, not after the first complaint.\n\nProve it, then keep proving it\n\nElimination has to be verified. That means fatty acid analysis of finished products, specifications that limit trans fat in every incoming oil, and supplier checks, so that partially hydrogenated material does not return through a minor ingredient. Shelf-life testing deserves the same discipline. A fat that performs on day one may change its texture after weeks in a warm warehouse, as its crystals slowly change form. Test at the temperatures your products really see, not only in the laboratory.\n\nCheck the current legal limit in every market you sell into, because the limits differ. \n\nJudge it on cost-in-use, not price per tonne\n\nA replacement fat may cost more per tonne and still cost less in use, if it runs faster, wastes less or allows a lower dose. Judge the change on the whole cost, across your plant and your customer’s.\n\nIn preparation (not yet published, no content to quote): Bleaching earth: a small input with a big effect on oil quality and cost; Cocoa butter equivalents in India: the opportunity and the traps; Passing a multinational customer’s pre-approval audit: what auditors actually look for; Refining palm oil at −25 °C: lessons from a cold-climate refinery.\n\n## Contact\nEmail: office@kokreaters.com. WhatsApp: +31 6 2215 7910. LinkedIn: linkedin.com/in/ganeayshkulkarni.\nThe contact form is on /contact (\"Let’s create something together.\"). Tell us a little about your challenge. Ganeaysh reads every message personally and replies within one working day.\nTell us about your product, your plant or your decision. The first conversation is free of charge.\nFields the form asks for: Name, Company, Role, Email, Phone or WhatsApp, Country, Area of interest, Message, How did you hear about us?.\n";
